  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The implementation covers the whole requirement, but one explicit MUST NOT has no test.\n\n**Implementation.** `setup::run` in `src/setup.rs` (marked `{impl}`) does each of these:\n- It adds the dev dependency with `cargo add --dev habeas`, passing `--path` when given. It skips this if the dev dependency already exists.\n- It configures the `habeas mdbook` preprocessor with the `html` renderer.\n- It adds the `habeas.css` stylesheet entry to `additional-css`, without duplicating it.\n- It sets `output.html.fold.enable = true` only when the book has not already set it.\n- It creates `tests/habeas.rs` containing `habeas::validate()`.\n\n`write_new_or_same` and the pre-check loop in `run` refuse to overwrite a `habeas.css` or `tests/habeas.rs` whose content differs. They do this before any modification. The code never touches agent skills. `main.rs` dispatches `Command::Setup` to `setup::run` with an `{impl}` marker. The `is_configured` function is `{cite}` only, but it is a status check for the hook, not part of this requirement's behavior.\n\n**Tests.** `tests/setup.rs` covers these points:\n- `setup_is_repeatable` runs setup twice. It checks that the dev dependency, preprocessor, stylesheet and test file are in place, that the stylesheet entry is not duplicated, that `enable` appears once, and that no `.agents/skills` directory is created.\n- `setup_keeps_an_existing_fold_setting` covers the \"unless the book already sets it\" case in three variants.\n- `setup_preserves_existing_agent_skills` checks that an existing custom skill file is left alone.\n- `setup_rejects_invalid_book_configuration` and `setup_uses_src_when_book_table_or_source_setting_is_absent` cover invalid and absent book settings.\n- `tests/cli.rs` rejects `--install-skills`.\n\n**Gap.** I found no test that runs setup when `tests/habeas.rs` or the `habeas.css` stylesheet already exists with different content. Such a test would assert that setup fails and leaves the file untouched. A search of `tests/` for \"different content\" and for writes to those files found none. This is a stated MUST NOT, and the refusal branches in `run` and `write_new_or_same` are never exercised. There is no FIXME acknowledging the gap, so the result is false rather than FIXME.",
